Before you complain bitterly about your client, you could consider
that it may be doing some additional sanity checks beyond those [not]
used by OE.

The beginning of a legitimate UUENCODE attachment should be
something like:  begin 644 test.txt

If the numeric umask is missing or if there is extra text after
the file name, a client might decide not to treat this as introducing
an attachment.

Or if the next line doesn't contain something that is a valid UUENCODE
line (should have encoded line length flush left and 4 encodes 3
binary data out to that encode line length on remainder of line) then
the client might decide to treat it as plain text and give up on
trying to decode it as an attachment.

I posted a uuencoded attachment.  Interpreting it as a uuencoded
attachment is arguably proper.  (Or one could take the purist view
that text is text and should be displayed as such).

It is accidentally interpreting something that looks a lot like
a uuencoded attachment as a uuencoded attachment that is the
problematic behavior.

